item.text:
**Reused Evidence**
No prior evidence items genuinely applied to 5B, so there are no `REUSE_JSON` lines.

**New Evidence**
- Official 2022 policy agenda: target growth, reform, opening-up, and welfare.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"China releases full text of Government Work Report","publisher":"english.www.gov.cn / Xinhua","publication_date":"2022-03-12","url":"https://english.www.gov.cn/news/topnews/202203/12/content_WS622c98a8c6d09c94e48a6904.html","claim":"The 2022 Government Work Report set targets of around 5.5 percent GDP growth, over 11 million new urban jobs, stable macroeconomic performance, deeper reform, higher-standard opening up, innovation, and improved wellbeing.","locator":"lines 57-96","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary government program text is strong for announced intent and policy direction, but weaker on realized effect.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Target-year agenda for 2022, published March 12, 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but material. It was delivered by Premier Li Keqiang for the Xi-led State Council, so it reflects top-state economic direction rather than Xi's sole authorship.","contrary_evidence":["The report is aspirational and does not itself show implementation.","Actual 2022 growth later landed well below the 5.5 percent target."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.3","5B.6","5B.7","5B.10"]}

- State Council stabilization package: broad macro and SME support.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"China issues policy package to stabilize economy","publisher":"english.www.gov.cn","publication_date":"2022-05-31","url":"https://english.www.gov.cn/policies/latestreleases/202205/31/content_WS6295ca6ec6d02e533532b89b.html","claim":"The State Council issued a 33-measure package to stabilize the economy, including VAT refunds, faster local bond use, SME support, loan relief for affected borrowers, expanded investment, and support for foreign-funded projects.","locator":"lines 45-52","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary policy announcement with concrete instruments and implementation instructions.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-period 2022 policy response, issued May 31, 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Xi-era State Council rather than Xi personally; this is strong evidence of regime-level policy direction and response capacity.","contrary_evidence":["This is a response to slowdown and pandemic damage, not proof that underlying structural problems were solved.","The package's effectiveness has to be judged against later outcome data."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.6","5B.9","5B.10"]}

- Monetary easing and liquidity support through the year.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 Fourth Quarter China Monetary Policy Execution Report","publisher":"People's Bank of China","publication_date":"2023-02-24","url":"https://www.pbc.gov.cn/zhengcehuobisi/125207/125227/125957/4584071/d7e45aa7d98c4664a9a6c2393d16787d/index.html","claim":"The PBOC said 2022 featured two reserve-requirement cuts, more than 1 trillion yuan of long-term liquidity release, stable money and credit growth, lower financing costs, and a basically stable RMB exchange rate.","locator":"content summary paragraph","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary central-bank summary is strong for the reported instruments and aggregate metrics, but it is still an official self-assessment.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective full-year 2022 report published in early 2023.","ruler_attribution":"Attribution is to the Xi-era party-state and monetary authorities, not to Xi alone; it still shows ruler-period macro management choices.","contrary_evidence":["This is an official success framing, not an independent evaluation.","The same year still ended with weak growth and a stressed property sector."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.9","5B.10"]}

- Open-up step: foreign investment negative list shortened again.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"China shortens negative lists for foreign investment 5th year in a row","publisher":"english.www.gov.cn","publication_date":"2022-01-08","url":"https://english.www.gov.cn/news/videos/202201/08/content_WS61d90203c6d09c94e48a3564.html","claim":"China cut the nationwide and free-trade-zone foreign-investment negative lists to 31 and 27 items, respectively, and framed the move as opening the economy and treating foreign and domestic investment more equally.","locator":"lines 45-47","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary policy announcement with clear, date-stamped rule changes.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Within target year 2022, effective Jan. 1, 2022.","ruler_attribution":"State-level opening-up policy under Xi-led top leadership; the measure is real, but it does not prove broader market fairness by itself.","contrary_evidence":["The measure is selective liberalization, not a full market opening.","Later World Bank and IMF material still described regulatory uncertainty and credit distortions."],"lenses":["5B.4","5B.10"]}

- Small-business and self-employed support.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"China unveils regulation on boosting development of self-employed businesses","publisher":"english.www.gov.cn","publication_date":"2022-10-25","url":"https://english.www.gov.cn/policies/latestreleases/202210/25/content_WS63588253c6d0a757729e1c60.html","claim":"A State Council regulation promised to simplify administrative procedures, improve the business environment, and protect the lawful rights and interests of self-employed businesses through finance, tax, social security, and market-access support.","locator":"lines 48-50","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary rule text with concrete administrative changes and support channels.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-period 2022 policy action, effective Nov. 1, 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Xi-era State Council; it supports claims about the ruler's treatment of small private operators, though implementation was still uneven.","contrary_evidence":["Helpful regulation does not erase broader private-sector pressure in 2022.","The business environment remained weak in the property and tech sectors."],"lenses":["5B.4","5B.6","5B.8","5B.10"]}

- Data as a production factor and a new institutional framework.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"China unveils measures to build basic systems for data","publisher":"english.www.gov.cn","publication_date":"2022-12-19","url":"https://english.www.gov.cn/policies/latestreleases/202212/19/content_WS63a17f7dc6d0a757729e49bd.html","claim":"A CPC Central Committee and State Council document laid out 20 key measures to build basic systems for data, including property, circulation-and-trading, income-distribution, and governance systems, to better use data resources and empower the real economy.","locator":"search result text and lead paragraphs","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary policy document, strong on intended institutional design, less direct on realized economic effect.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Late-2022 target-period policy document.","ruler_attribution":"Directly within the Xi-led party-state architecture; this is stronger as evidence of strategic direction than of immediate implementation.","contrary_evidence":["Building systems for data can also expand state control, not just productivity.","The document is about design, not proven economic payoff."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.7","5B.8","5B.10"]}

- Zero-COVID policy correction toward targeted implementation.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"COVID-19 response further optimized with 10 new measures","publisher":"english.www.gov.cn","publication_date":"2022-12-08","url":"https://english.www.gov.cn/statecouncil/ministries/202212/08/content_WS63913c92c6d0a757729e4135.html","claim":"China released 10 new COVID measures to replace oversimplified one-size-fits-all approaches with more science-based and targeted controls, explicitly ordering localities to correct excessive policy steps.","locator":"lines 45-48","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary policy announcement with concrete administrative instructions.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-period 2022 policy correction, published Dec. 8, 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Strongly attributable to the Xi-led party-state because it represents the top-level pivot in pandemic governance, though implementation ran through health and local authorities.","contrary_evidence":["The pivot was late and followed major economic and social damage.","The policy change does not erase the earlier costs of strict zero-COVID enforcement."],"lenses":["5B.7","5B.9","5B.10"]}

- Housing-sector rescue and delivery of pre-sold homes.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"China unveils financial measures to bolster housing market","publisher":"english.scio.gov.cn / Xinhua","publication_date":"2022-11-24","url":"https://english.scio.gov.cn/pressroom/2022-11/24/content_78534777.htm","claim":"China's financial authorities rolled out 16 steps to stabilize real-estate financing, support reasonable mortgage demand, and ensure delivery of pre-sold housing projects.","locator":"opening paragraphs","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary policy announcement with a clearly specified policy package and purpose.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-period 2022 housing-sector response.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Xi-era financial and regulatory apparatus rather than Xi alone; it is still direct ruler-period policy evidence.","contrary_evidence":["This is damage control after a severe property downturn, not evidence that the underlying crisis was avoided.","The property market still weakened sharply in 2022."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.9","5B.10"]}

- Xi's 20th Congress report: high-quality development, common prosperity, opening-up, innovation.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Xi Jinping's report to the 20th National Congress of the CPC","publisher":"app.www.gov.cn / Xinhua","publication_date":"2022-10-25","url":"https://app.www.gov.cn/govdata/gov/202210/25/493662/article.html","claim":"Xi said China should pursue high-quality development, improve the distribution system, widen high-standard opening-up, strengthen innovation and talent policy, and make progress toward common prosperity.","locator":"lines 39-46 and 124-133","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct Xi-authored strategic report is strong for stated intent and self-assessed trajectory, though it remains a self-report.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Target-period strategic address delivered Oct. 16, 2022 and published Oct. 25, 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Xi as party leader and principal speaker.","contrary_evidence":["This is a self-report and mixes aspiration with retrospective claims.","Independent macro sources still described serious slowdown, property stress, and policy uncertainty in 2022."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.6","5B.8","5B.10"]}

- Xi's top leadership in 2022, with a team dominated by allies.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Factbox-China's new political elites and their connection to Xi","publisher":"Reuters via MarketScreener","publication_date":"2022-10-24","url":"https://www.marketscreener.com/news/latest/Factbox-China-s-new-political-elites-and-their-connection-to-Xi-42069587/","claim":"Reuters reported that Xi secured a third term and unveiled a new leadership team dominated by allies, including the entire seven-member Politburo Standing Committee.","locator":"lines 821-825","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters factbox is strong contemporaneous reporting on elite composition and political control.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-period leadership change in October 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Xi because it concerns his chosen top team and the consolidation of his personal network.","contrary_evidence":["Some new officials were technocrats, not pure ideologues.","A loyalist-heavy team does not by itself prove economic incompetence, only reduced independence."],"lenses":["5B.2","5B.7","5B.10"]}

- World Bank assessment of the 2022 slowdown and reform needs.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"China Economic Update - December 2022","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-12","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/country/china/publication/china-economic-update-december-2022","claim":"The World Bank said China's growth was expected to slow to 2.7 percent in 2022 amid pandemic disruptions and real-estate stress, and it called for deeper structural reforms, predictable regulation, better credit access, and more support for youth employment.","locator":"key messages","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"High-quality independent macro assessment with explicit policy recommendations and date fit.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-year 2022 publication assessing full-year conditions.","ruler_attribution":"This is not a Xi-only causal finding, but it is strong independent context for the Xi-era policy environment and the consequences of those choices.","contrary_evidence":["It is a forecast and policy recommendation, not a direct ruling on Xi's intent.","Part of the slowdown also came from global shocks and pandemic dynamics."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.4","5B.7","5B.9","5B.10"]}

- IMF assessment of zero-COVID, property crisis, and policy response.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"People's Republic of China: 2022 Article IV Consultation","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2023-02-02","url":"https://www.elibrary.imf.org/view/journals/002/2023/067/article-A001-en.xml","claim":"IMF staff said China's 2022 growth would be below global growth for the first time in over 40 years, with zero-COVID restrictions, the deep property-sector contraction, and weak global demand weighing on the economy, and it described support for unfinished housing and mortgage relief among the policy responses.","locator":"abstract and context section","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent macro surveillance with clear causation analysis and detailed policy discussion.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective 2022 IMF surveillance published after the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Not direct personal attribution, but it strongly assesses the Xi-led policy regime and its macro consequences.","contrary_evidence":["This is staff analysis, not a binding legal finding.","The IMF also recognizes that some support measures were introduced in response."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.4","5B.7","5B.9","5B.10"]}

- Full-year 2022 national outcome data: growth, investment, exports, inflation, unemployment, and rural/urban split.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"National Economy Withstood Pressure and Reached a New Level in 2022","publisher":"National Bureau of Statistics of China","publication_date":"2023-01-17","url":"https://www.stats.gov.cn/english/PressRelease/202301/t20230117_1892094.html?from=%25252525252525252540","claim":"The NBS reported 3.0 percent GDP growth in 2022, 5.1 percent fixed-asset investment growth, a 10.0 percent fall in real-estate development investment, 10.5 percent export growth, 2.0 percent CPI inflation, a December youth unemployment rate of 16.7 percent, and rural disposable-income growth faster than urban growth.","locator":"lines 52-54, 68-81","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary statistical release with full-year outcome data; official statistics still require caution, but this is the core national dataset.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Full-year 2022 outcome release published in January 2023.","ruler_attribution":"These are country outcomes, not automatic Xi credit or blame, but they are the main end-of-year economic results under his rule.","contrary_evidence":["Official statistics may understate private-sector weakness.","National averages conceal regional and distributional stress, especially in the property and youth-labor markets."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}

- Household income and consumption outcomes.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Households' Income and Consumption Expenditure in 2022","publisher":"National Bureau of Statistics of China","publication_date":"2023-01-18","url":"https://www.stats.gov.cn/english/PressRelease/202301/t20230118_1892303.html","claim":"The NBS reported that real per-capita disposable income rose 2.9 percent in 2022, rural income grew faster than urban income, and real per-capita consumption fell 0.2 percent, with the median income still below the mean.","locator":"lines 21-24 and 29-32","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary household survey release with detailed distributional measures, though still official data.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Full-year 2022 household outcomes published immediately after year end.","ruler_attribution":"Country-level household outcomes are only partially attributable to Xi, but they are the best official evidence of household welfare under his 2022 rule.","contrary_evidence":["Urban households' real income growth was only 1.9 percent and real consumption fell.","The release does not isolate ruler-specific causality from pandemic and macro conditions."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}

- R&D and technology investment expanded.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 National R&D Expenditure Statistics Bulletin","publisher":"National Bureau of Statistics of China / Ministry of Science and Technology / Ministry of Finance","publication_date":"2023-09-18","url":"https://www.stats.gov.cn/sj/zxfb/202309/t20230918_1942920.html","claim":"China's 2022 R&D spending reached 30,782.9 billion yuan, up 10.1 percent year on year, with intensity at 2.54 percent of GDP and basic research growing 11.4 percent.","locator":"lines 623-630 and 636","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary statistical bulletin with a clear full-year investment measure, though subject to official-data caveats.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Full-year 2022 report published later in 2023.","ruler_attribution":"This reflects Xi-era priorities for technology and innovation, though the R&D system is also shaped by ministries, firms, and provinces.","contrary_evidence":["Higher R&D spending does not guarantee efficient allocation or broad prosperity.","It does not by itself prove that innovation policy improved household welfare in 2022."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.10"]}

- Financial-sector supervision and anti-corruption claim.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Press conference on reform and development of China's financial sector since the 18th CPC National Congress","publisher":"english.scio.gov.cn","publication_date":"2022-06-29","url":"https://english.scio.gov.cn/m/pressroom/2022-06/29/content_78296373_13.htm","claim":"Officials said financial supervision had been tightened, more than 124 stockholders who breached laws or regulations had been disclosed, and corruption behind financial risks had been investigated and punished.","locator":"press-conference summary","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary official account with concrete enforcement figures, but it is still a self-justifying report from the sector it describes.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Target-year 2022 press conference summarizing the Xi era and ongoing 2022 reform posture.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Xi-era party-state financial governance model, though specific cases were handled by regulators and discipline bodies.","contrary_evidence":["This is an official self-report and may overstate effectiveness.","A crackdown can reflect political discipline as well as genuine anti-corruption."],"lenses":["5B.5","5B.3","5B.7"]}

**Search and Rejection Summary**
I reviewed roughly 30 documents across official Chinese policy releases, NBS statistical releases, the PBOC, the IMF, the World Bank, Reuters, and OECD-style structural commentary. I kept 16 non-duplicative URLs that collectively cover macro stabilization, monetary easing, housing rescue, opening-up, small-business support, data-policy design, COVID policy correction, leadership composition, household outcomes, R&D, and anti-corruption.

I rejected or de-prioritized:
- Duplicate Reuters syndications and mirrors of the same underlying story, especially on the leadership reshuffle, property rescue, and COVID rollback.
- Out-of-period follow-ups from 2023-2025 that were useful only as retrospectives but did not add target-year 2022 ruler-period detail.
- Broad China-economy commentary without a clear 2022 ruler-attributed policy claim.
- Background pieces on SOEs, competition, or productivity that repeated the same structural critique already covered by the IMF and World Bank.
- Structured dataset re-fetches already covered by the local priors, including WDI-style growth, income, and spending series.

**Remaining Gaps**
- Direct evidence of Xi personally choosing specific macro instruments is still limited; many concrete 2022 measures are formally State Council, PBOC, NDRC, or regulator actions within a Xi-led system.
- Distributional evidence is still mostly household averages and rural-vs-urban splits; I do not yet have strong independent evidence on wealth concentration, Gini movement, or regional divergence beyond those proxies.
- The evidence on appointments is strong for loyalist consolidation, but weaker on whether any economic technocrats retained meaningful autonomy inside the 2022 system.
- Anti-corruption and favoritism evidence is still mostly official self-reporting; more independent work on finance, SOEs, and local patronage would help.
- If you want, the next useful search would be on independent business-confidence surveys, FDI flows, and 2022 property-sector distress transmission to households and local government finance.
